# Load Balancer Configuration File for EC2 Deployment
# Format: host:port:weight
# Lines starting with # are comments
# Empty lines are ignored

# ============================================
# Configuration Examples
# ============================================

# Example 1: Backends on same EC2 instance (for testing)
# Uncomment and use if all backends run on the same instance
# 127.0.0.1:9001:3
# 127.0.0.1:9002:2
# 127.0.0.1:9003:1

# Example 2: Backends on different EC2 instances (same VPC - use private IPs)
# Replace with your actual backend private IPs
# 10.0.1.10:8080:3
# 10.0.1.11:8080:2
# 10.0.1.12:8080:1

# Example 3: Backends with public IPs (different VPCs or regions)
# Replace with your actual backend public IPs
# 54.123.45.67:8080:3
# 54.123.45.68:8080:2
# 54.123.45.69:8080:1

# Example 4: Using domain names/URLs (RECOMMENDED for production)
# The load balancer supports both IP addresses and domain names
# Replace with your actual backend domain names
# api1.example.com:8080:3
# api2.example.com:8080:2
# api3.example.com:8080:1

# Example 5: Production URLs with HTTPS (port 443)
# api.production.com:443:3
# api2.production.com:443:2
# api3.production.com:443:1

# Example 6: Mixed configuration (local + remote + domains)
# 127.0.0.1:9001:3
# 10.0.1.20:8080:2
# api.example.com:8080:1

# ============================================
# Your Configuration
# ============================================
# Edit below with your actual backend servers:

# Backend 1 (50% of traffic - weight 3)
# YOUR_BACKEND_1_IP:YOUR_BACKEND_1_PORT:3

# Backend 2 (33% of traffic - weight 2)
# YOUR_BACKEND_2_IP:YOUR_BACKEND_2_PORT:2

# Backend 3 (17% of traffic - weight 1)
# YOUR_BACKEND_3_IP:YOUR_BACKEND_3_PORT:1

# ============================================
# Weight Distribution Guide
# ============================================
# Weights determine traffic distribution:
# - 3:2:1 = 50% : 33% : 17%
# - 5:3:2 = 50% : 30% : 20%
# - 1:1:1 = 33% : 33% : 33% (equal)
# - 10:5:1 = 62.5% : 31.25% : 6.25%

# ============================================
# Notes
# ============================================
# - The load balancer supports BOTH IP addresses AND domain names/URLs
# - Domain names are recommended for production (easier to manage, can change IPs)
# - Use private IPs for backends in same VPC (faster, no data transfer costs)
# - Use public IPs or domain names for backends in different VPCs or regions
# - Ensure security groups allow traffic from load balancer to backends
# - Ports must be accessible from load balancer instance
# - Weights must be between 1 and 100
# - Domain names will be resolved via DNS (make sure DNS is configured correctly)

